<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Computer Graphics Microproject</div>
    <div class="slide-title">3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 40pt;">
          <h2 style="font-size: 24pt; color: #007acc; margin-bottom: 12pt;">Using Polygon Normals, Cross Products & Dot Products</h2>
          <p style="font-size: 15pt; color: #555; margin-bottom: 30pt;">Real-Time Surface Orientation Detection for Industrial Quality Control in Blender 5.0.1</p>
          <div style="display: inline-block; text-align: left; background: #f0f4f8; padding: 20pt 30pt; border-radius: 8pt; border: 1pt solid #ccc;">
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; margin: 4pt 0;"><strong>Environment:</strong> Blender 5.0.1 Python API (bpy)</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; margin: 4pt 0;"><strong>Core Concept:</strong> Surface Normals & Vector Dot Product</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; margin: 4pt 0;"><strong>CAD Assembly:</strong> Industrial Flanged Valve Housing (826 Polygons)</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; margin: 4pt 0;"><strong>Academic Year:</strong> 2025–2026</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> Good morning professors. Welcome to our presentation on Real-Time 3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection Using Polygon Normals in Blender 5.0.1.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 1 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 1 • Motivation</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Industrial Problem Statement & Motivation</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; margin-top: 20pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">The Manufacturing Challenge</h3>
            <ul style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.8; margin-top: 10pt;">
              <li>In smart factories (Industry 4.0), robotic optical cameras and laser scanners inspect mechanical parts before assembly.</li>
              <li>Parts rotate on assembly lines; surface visibility changes continuously.</li>
              <li>Sensors <strong>cannot measure surfaces facing away</strong>, and glancing surfaces produce severe laser scatter artifacts.</li>
              <li><strong>Need:</strong> A real-time system that determines scan-ready surfaces geometrically before physical scanning begins.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #f8fafc; padding: 20pt; border-radius: 8pt; border: 1pt solid #cbd5e1;">
            <h3 style="color: #2e7d32; font-size: 16pt;">Key Objectives</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 8pt;">✓ Calculate polygon normals via <strong>vector cross products</strong>.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.7;">✓ Transform normals to world space via <strong>normal matrix (M<sup>-1</sup>)<sup>T</sup></strong>.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.7;">✓ Classify faces via <strong>dot product</strong>: Front (Green), Back (Red), Side (Yellow).</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.7;">✓ Provide sidebar UI controls and <strong>export CSV quality logs</strong>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> Robotic optical scanners cannot scan surfaces pointing away, and glancing angles create severe distortion. Our software calculates polygon orientation dynamically to determine scan-ready surfaces.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 2 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 2 • Computational Geometry</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Core CG Concept: Surface Normals & Cross Product</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; margin-top: 20pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">What is a Surface Normal?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.6;">An imaginary unit vector perpendicular (90°) to a polygon's planar facet.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 10pt;">Following the <strong>Right-Hand Rule</strong>, counter-clockwise (CCW) vertex winding defines the outward normal direction.</p>
            
            <div style="background: #eef2f6; border-left: 4pt solid #007acc; padding: 12pt; font-family: monospace; font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 14pt;">
              E1 = P1 - P0<br>
              E2 = P2 - P0<br>
              N = E1 × E2<br>
              N_unit = N / ||N||
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #f8fafc; padding: 20pt; border-radius: 8pt; border: 1pt solid #cbd5e1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">Cross Product Expansion</h3>
            <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1pt solid #ccc; padding: 12pt; font-family: monospace; font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 10pt;">
              Nx = E1_y · E2_z - E1_z · E2_y<br>
              Ny = E1_z · E2_x - E1_x · E2_z<br>
              Nz = E1_x · E2_y - E1_y · E2_x
            </div>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; color: #666; margin-top: 12pt;">
              Evaluated across all 826 polygons in the industrial CAD component to guarantee exact outward perpendiculars.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> A surface normal is a perpendicular unit vector. We compute it from first principles using the vector cross product of two edge vectors sharing a vertex following the right-hand rule.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 3 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 2 • Linear Algebra</div>
    <div class="slide-title">World Transformation: The Normal Matrix (M^-1)^T</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; margin-top: 20pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #c62828; font-size: 16pt;">The Non-Uniform Scale Problem</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.6;">If an object is transformed by model matrix M with non-uniform scaling (e.g. scale Y = 3), multiplying the normal directly by M distorts the vector, destroying 90° orthogonality!</p>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 10pt;">Surface normals are <strong>covariant vectors</strong> (representing planes), not spatial position points.</p>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #f8fafc; padding: 20pt; border-radius: 8pt; border: 1pt solid #cbd5e1;">
            <h3 style="color: #2e7d32; font-size: 16pt;">The Mathematical Solution</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.6;">To preserve strict orthogonality, normals must be transformed using the <strong>inverse-transpose of the upper 3×3 model matrix</strong>:</p>
            <div style="background: #ffffff; border-left: 4pt solid #2e7d32; padding: 12pt; font-family: monospace; font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 10pt;">
              M_normal = (M_3x3^-1)^T<br>
              N_world = normalize( M_normal @ N_local )
            </div>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; color: #2e7d32; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 10pt;">
              ✓ Verified: Normal remains strictly orthogonal to surface edges under arbitrary affine transforms.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> A classic trap in 3D graphics is multiplying normals directly by the model matrix. Under non-uniform scaling, that breaks orthogonality. Our code implements the inverse-transpose matrix, guaranteeing exact perpendicularity.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 4 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 2 • Classification Algorithm</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Dot Product Orientation Classification</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <p style="font-size: 13pt; text-align: center; margin-top: 10pt;">
          Let V be the unit inspection vector. The dot product yields the cosine of the spatial angle θ:
        </p>
        <div style="background: #eef2f6; border: 1pt solid #cbd5e1; padding: 12pt; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14pt; text-align: center; width: 60%; margin: 12pt auto;">
          D = N_world · V = Nx·Vx + Ny·Vy + Nz·Vz = cos(θ)
        </div>

        <div style="display: flex; gap: 20pt; margin-top: 16pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #e8f5e9; border: 2pt solid #2e7d32; border-radius: 8pt; padding: 16pt; text-align: center;">
            <h3 style="color: #2e7d32; font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 6pt;">FRONT-FACING (Green)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 16pt; font-weight: bold; color: #2e7d32;">D &gt; +0.30</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 6pt;">Angle θ &lt; 72.5°</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt; color: #555; margin-top: 6pt;">Surfaces pointing directly towards the sensor. Scan-ready.</p>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #ffebee; border: 2pt solid #c62828; border-radius: 8pt; padding: 16pt; text-align: center;">
            <h3 style="color: #c62828; font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 6pt;">BACK-FACING (Red)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 16pt; font-weight: bold; color: #c62828;">D &lt; -0.30</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 6pt;">Angle θ &gt; 107.5°</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt; color: #555; margin-top: 6pt;">Surfaces pointing away from the sensor. Hidden from view.</p>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #fffde7; border: 2pt solid #f57f17; border-radius: 8pt; padding: 16pt; text-align: center;">
            <h3 style="color: #f57f17; font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 6pt;">SIDE-FACING (Yellow)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 16pt; font-weight: bold; color: #f57f17;">|D| ≤ 0.30</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 6pt;">72.5° ≤ θ ≤ 107.5°</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt; color: #555; margin-top: 6pt;">Glancing edge-on surfaces prone to optical reflection artifacts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> By calculating the dot product with the inspection vector, we get the cosine of the angle. With an adjustable threshold of 0.30, every face is unambiguously classified into Front (Green), Back (Red), or Side (Yellow).
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 5 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 4 • 3D Modeling</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Industrial CAD Component Architecture</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; align-items: center; margin-top: 10pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">Flanged Pipe Valve Housing</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 8pt;">
              Modeled in BMesh to provide a realistic mixture of flat, curved, angled, and multi-directional facets:
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 6pt;">
              <li><strong>Valve Housing (400 faces):</strong> Cylindrical body, pipe section, 2 flanges, 4 stiffener gussets.</li>
              <li><strong>Base Plate (70 faces):</strong> Machined steel slab with corner mounting pads.</li>
              <li><strong>Top Cover (68 faces):</strong> Circular hatch with knurled cap & hex nut.</li>
              <li><strong>Hex Fasteners (288 faces):</strong> 22 precision hex bolts with circular washers.</li>
            </ul>
            <div style="background: #f0f4f8; padding: 10pt; border-radius: 6pt; margin-top: 12pt;">
              <strong>Total Geometry:</strong> 826 Polygons | 1,426 Vertices
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; text-align: center;">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1pt solid #ccc; border-radius: 6pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);">
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: #666; margin-top: 4pt; font-style: italic;">Original CAD model with realistic PBR metallic shaders.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> Here is our 3D component with original PBR materials. It contains 826 polygons with cylindrical pipe bodies, flat flanges, 45-degree gussets, and 22 hex bolts with washers.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 6 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 5 • Visual Results</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Real-Time Inspection & Normal Arrows</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; align-items: center; margin-top: 10pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1; text-align: center;">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1pt solid #ccc; border-radius: 6pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);">
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: #666; margin-top: 4pt; font-style: italic;">Real-time inspection view with green/red/yellow faces & cyan normal arrows.</p>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">Visual Features Implemented:</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 12.5pt; margin-top: 8pt;"><span style="color: #2e7d32; font-weight: bold;">● Green:</span> Front-facing polygons (D &gt; +0.30)</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12.5pt; margin-top: 6pt;"><span style="color: #c62828; font-weight: bold;">● Red:</span> Back-facing polygons (D &lt; -0.30)</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12.5pt; margin-top: 6pt;"><span style="color: #f57f17; font-weight: bold;">● Yellow:</span> Glancing side-facing polygons (|D| ≤ 0.30)</p>
            <p style="font-size: 12.5pt; margin-top: 6pt;"><span style="color: #00bcd4; font-weight: bold;">● Cyan Arrows:</span> 413 3D normal vector indicators at polygon centers.</p>
            
            <div style="background: #e0f7fa; border-left: 4pt solid #00bcd4; padding: 10pt; border-radius: 6pt; margin-top: 14pt;">
              <h4 style="color: #00838f; margin: 0 0 4pt 0;">Unified Mesh Architecture:</h4>
              <p style="font-size: 11pt; color: #006064; margin: 0;">
                All 413 arrows are batched into a <strong>single draw-call mesh</strong> (Normal_Vectors_Visualizer), maintaining ~120 FPS in the viewport!
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> Here is the active inspection state. Green surfaces face the sensor, red face away, and yellow are glancing. The cyan arrows show the exact calculated normal perpendiculars.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 7 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 6 • Multi-Angle Verification</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Multi-Angle Orientation Presets</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; align-items: center; margin-top: 10pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1; text-align: center;">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1pt solid #ccc; border-radius: 6pt; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);">
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: #666; margin-top: 4pt; font-style: italic;">Top View (+Z) inspection. Horizontal faces turn green, cylinder walls turn yellow.</p>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1;">
            <h3 style="color: #007acc; font-size: 16pt;">Testing Direction +Z (Top View):</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 8pt;">
              When the inspection vector changes to (0, 0, 1):
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 6pt;">
              <li>All horizontal top surfaces (top cover, flange tops, base plate top) turn <strong>Green</strong>.</li>
              <li>All downward-pointing bottom surfaces turn <strong>Red</strong>.</li>
              <li>All vertical cylindrical walls turn <strong>Yellow</strong> (90° to +Z).</li>
            </ul>
            <div style="background: #f1f8e9; border: 1pt solid #c5e1a5; padding: 12pt; border-radius: 6pt; margin-top: 12pt;">
              <p style="margin: 3pt 0;"><strong>Front-Facing:</strong> 161 (19.5%)</p>
              <p style="margin: 3pt 0;"><strong>Back-Facing:</strong> 161 (19.5%)</p>
              <p style="margin: 3pt 0;"><strong>Side-Facing:</strong> 504 (61.0%)</p>
              <p style="margin: 4pt 0; color: #2e7d32; font-weight: bold;">✓ Total: 826 / 826 (100.0% Verified)</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> When switching to the +Z top-down view, all horizontal faces turn Green, bottom faces turn Red, and the vertical walls turn Yellow. The face count strictly sums to 826.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
