<div class="slide-container">
  <div>
    <div class="slide-badge">Chapter 7 • Discussion</div>
    <div class="slide-title">Crucial Distinction & Industrial Value</div>
  </div>
  <div class="slide-body">
    
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 30pt; margin-top: 16pt;">
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #f0fdf4; border: 2pt solid #22c55e; border-radius: 8pt; padding: 16pt;">
            <h3 style="color: #15803d; font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 0;">What This Project DOES:</h3>
            <ul style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 8pt;">
              <li>Accurately evaluates <strong>geometric surface orientation</strong> relative to an optical sensor vector.</li>
              <li>Identifies which surface facets are scan-ready, hidden, or at glancing angles.</li>
              <li>Generates audit trails in CSV format for engineering traceability.</li>
              <li>Maintains non-destructive CAD geometry integrity.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div style="flex: 1; background: #fef2f2; border: 2pt solid #ef4444; border-radius: 8pt; padding: 16pt;">
            <h3 style="color: #b91c1c; font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 0;">What This Project DOES NOT Do:</h3>
            <ul style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 8pt;">
              <li>Does not detect microscopic metallurgical flaws (cracks, scratches, porosity, corrosion).</li>
              <li>Does not measure material hardness or thickness.</li>
            </ul>
            <div style="background: #ffffff; border-left: 4pt solid #b91c1c; padding: 8pt; margin-top: 12pt; font-size: 11pt; color: #444;">
              <strong>Industrial Value:</strong> Geometric orientation inspection is the essential prerequisite that tells robotic cameras which surfaces to scan!
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="speaker-notes-box">
      <strong>Speaker Notes:</strong> An important distinction to mention: our software inspects geometric surface orientation, not physical defects like cracks. In factory automation, knowing orientation is the critical first step before scanning.
    </div>
    <div class="slide-footer">
      <span>3D Industrial Component Surface Inspection | Computer Graphics Microproject</span>
      <span>Slide 11 of 12</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

